D={
"ottoman-empire":("The Ottoman Empire ruled Turks, Arabs, Greeks, Armenians, Jews, Kurds and Slavs. Istanbul had perhaps 700,000 people in the 16th century — one of the world's largest cities — with covered bazaars, coffee houses, public fountains and hamams endowed by charitable foundations (waqf). Peasants farmed land held by timariot cavalrymen in return for military service, while Christian boys taken through the devshirme could rise to grand vizier. Non-Muslim communities ran their own religious, educational and family-law affairs under the millet system.","Ottoman culture blended Turkish, Persian, Arab and Byzantine traditions: calligraphy, miniature painting, Iznik tiles, carpets, Sufi music of the whirling Mevlevi dervishes, and poetry in Ottoman Turkish. Mimar Sinan's mosques, such as the Süleymaniye and Selimiye, define its architecture. Coffee arrived from Yemen in the 1550s, and coffee houses became centres of conversation, chess and Karagöz shadow-puppet theatre, sometimes banned as hotbeds of dissent."),
"safavid-iran":("Under Shah Abbas I, Isfahan grew to perhaps 600,000 people, with the vast Naqsh-e Jahan square used for polo, markets and ceremonies. He resettled thousands of Armenians in New Julfa to run Iran's silk trade, which linked Iran to Europe and India. Craftsmen produced carpets, ceramics and textiles for export, and caravanserais lined the trade roads.","Shah Ismail I imposed Twelver Shia Islam, converting most Iranians and creating a lasting distinction from Sunni Ottomans and Mughals; the mourning of Muharram became central. Persian miniature painting peaked with masters like Reza Abbasi, and Persian poetry and architecture influenced the Mughal court, where many Iranian artists and administrators went."),
"indus-valley-civilisation":("Indus cities like Mohenjo-daro had perhaps 40,000 people living in brick houses with bathrooms and wells, connected to covered street drains — a level of urban sanitation unmatched for millennia. Farmers grew wheat, barley, cotton and sesame; craftsmen made beads of carnelian, shell bangles, pottery and copper tools using standardised weights. Merchants traded with Mesopotamia, where Indus seals have been found. The absence of palaces, grand royal tombs or obvious weapons suggests a relatively egalitarian society.","Indus people used about 400 signs, the undeciphered Indus script, on seals with animals like the 'unicorn', bull and elephant. Figurines of a 'Mother Goddess', the 'Priest-King', the bronze 'Dancing Girl' and the 'Pashupati' seal suggest religious ideas some link to later Hinduism, such as possible yoga postures and reverence for the pipal tree. The Great Bath may have been used for ritual purification."),
"vedic-india":("Early Vedic people were semi-nomadic pastoralists who measured wealth in cattle; later they settled into villages of mud and thatch in the Ganga plain, farming barley and rice with iron ploughs. Society was organised in clans (jana) and families (kula) headed by men, but women like Gargi and Maitreyi took part in philosophical debates. Chariot races, gambling with dice and music were pastimes. Over time the varna system of Brahmins, Kshatriyas, Vaishyas and Shudras became more rigid.","Religion centred on fire sacrifices (yajnas) to Agni, Indra, Varuna and Soma, with hymns preserved orally in the four Vedas. The Upanishads introduced ideas of Brahman, atman, karma and rebirth. The ashrama system described stages of life — student, householder, forest dweller and renunciant."),
"magadha":("Magadha's people farmed wet rice in the fertile middle Ganga plain and mined iron from nearby hills. Cities like Rajagriha and Pataliputra grew with markets, guilds and punch-marked coins. Merchants and artisans formed guilds (shrenis).","Magadha was a centre of new religious movements: the Buddha and Mahavira preached there, and Ajivikas and Charvakas also debated. Many Buddhist sites, including Bodh Gaya, Rajgir and Nalanda, lie in Magadha."),
"maurya-empire":("Megasthenes described seven classes in Mauryan society, from philosophers and farmers to soldiers and officials, and noted that India had no slaves (though the Arthashastra mentions them). Farmers paid about a quarter of their produce; the state ran mines, forests and workshops and maintained roads with milestones and rest houses. Pataliputra was a huge city with wooden walls, 570 towers and 64 gates.","Ashoka's dhamma promoted non-violence, tolerance among sects, respect for elders and kindness to animals, and he banned some animal sacrifices. Buddhism spread to Sri Lanka and Central Asia, while Jainism and Brahmanical religion also flourished. Mauryan art includes polished sandstone pillars with animal capitals and the Didarganj Yakshi."),
"satavahana-dynasty":("Satavahana society included farmers, guilds of artisans and merchants, and seafarers who traded with Rome through ports like Sopara and Kalyan. Inscriptions record donations to Buddhist monasteries by merchants, craftsmen and women. Kings used their mothers' names, suggesting the importance of mothers.","The Satavahanas were Brahmanical but supported Buddhism generously; Prakrit literature such as the Gatha Saptashati, love poems attributed to King Hala, flourished. Amaravati and Karla show their art."),
"sangam-era-tamilakam":("Sangam poems describe five landscapes (tinai) — hills, forests, farmland, coast and desert — each with its people, occupations and moods of love. Farmers grew rice, fishermen worked the coast, and port cities like Muziris and Puhar traded pepper, pearls and muslin with Rome. Women poets such as Avvaiyar wrote, and Keeladi pottery inscriptions suggest widespread literacy.","Tamil culture celebrated love (akam) and war (puram) in poetry; warriors who died were honoured with hero stones. Murugan was the main deity, alongside Vedic, Buddhist and Jain influences."),
"kushan-empire":("The Kushan Empire was a crossroads where Greek, Persian, Indian, Chinese and steppe peoples traded; caravans carried silk, spices, horses and Roman gold. Cities like Taxila, Peshawar and Mathura grew rich.","Kushan coins show Greek, Iranian and Indian gods, reflecting tolerance. Gandhara art produced the first human images of the Buddha, with Greek-style drapery, while Mathura art developed independently."),
"gupta-empire":("Gupta India had rich towns with guilds, and a growing feudal system of land grants. Faxian (about 405) reported free hospitals, rest houses and that the 'chandalas' (untouchables) lived outside towns and had to announce their presence — evidence of caste discrimination. Women's position declined, with child marriage and sati appearing in some texts.","Classical Hinduism developed with temple worship of Vishnu and Shiva and the Puranas. Kalidasa's plays, the Kamasutra, the Panchatantra and the Natyashastra date from this era. Nalanda was founded, and Buddhist and Jain learning thrived."),
"pallava-dynasty":("Pallava Kanchipuram was a centre of learning with a ghatika (college). Ports like Mamallapuram traded with Southeast Asia.","Pallava kings supported Shaivism and Vaishnavism; the Bhakti poets (Nayanars and Alvars) composed Tamil hymns. Mamallapuram's carvings show daily life and myths."),
"chalukya-dynasty":("Chalukya society was agrarian, with villages paying taxes to feudatories. Aihole was a merchant guild centre.","Chalukyas built Hindu and Jain temples at Badami, Aihole and Pattadakal, mixing north and south styles."),
"rashtrakuta-dynasty":("Rashtrakuta society was agrarian and mercantile; Arab traders praised the kingdom.","They patronised Shaivism, Vaishnavism and Jainism; Kannada and Sanskrit literature flourished, and the Kailasa temple at Ellora was carved."),
"chola-empire":("Chola villages were self-governing: inscriptions at Uttaramerur describe election by lot (kudavolai) of committees for tanks, gardens and justice. Temples were the centres of economic life, employing dancers, musicians, cooks and accountants and lending money. Merchant guilds like the Ainnurruvar traded across Southeast Asia and China.","Cholas were devoted Shaivas who built the Brihadeeswarar and Gangaikonda Cholapuram temples. Chola bronze sculptures, especially Nataraja, are masterpieces. Bharatanatyam's ancestor, temple dance, flourished, and Kamban wrote his Tamil Ramayana."),
"pala-empire":("The Palas ruled a rich Bengal with rice farming and river trade.","The Palas supported Buddhist monasteries at Nalanda, Vikramashila and Somapura, and scholars like Atisha took Buddhism to Tibet."),
"delhi-sultanate":("Delhi grew into one of the largest cities in the Islamic world, with markets, mosques and madrasas. Peasants paid land revenue to iqtadars; Alauddin's price controls kept grain cheap for soldiers. Slaves could rise to power (the Mamluk sultans were former slaves).","Persian became the court language, and Hindustani emerged from mixing. Sufi saints like Nizamuddin Auliya attracted followers of all faiths; Amir Khusrau developed qawwali. Indo-Islamic architecture produced the Qutub Minar."),
"vijayanagara-empire":("Hampi had perhaps 500,000 people, with markets selling diamonds, horses and spices; travellers like Domingo Paes and Niccolò de' Conti described it as a city as large as Rome. Brahmins, warriors, merchants, artisans and devadasis made up society; nayakas controlled villages.","Vijayanagara patronised temple festivals, Carnatic music (Purandara Dasa), and Telugu, Kannada and Sanskrit literature. The Mahanavami festival displayed royal power."),
"ahom-kingdom":("The Ahom paik system required each adult man to serve the state in rotation. Wet rice farming and river transport sustained the economy.","Ahoms blended Tai traditions with Hinduism and recorded history in Buranjis."),
"mughal-empire":("Mughal India produced about a quarter of the world's manufacturing, mainly textiles. Peasants paid a third or more of their produce in revenue to mansabdars, while artisans in karkhanas produced luxury goods. Cities like Agra, Delhi and Lahore were among the world's largest, with bazaars, caravanserais and gardens. European travellers like Bernier described both splendour and peasant poverty.","Mughal culture blended Persian, Central Asian and Indian traditions: miniature painting, Mughal gardens (charbagh), the Taj Mahal, Hindustani music (Tansen), Urdu poetry, and cuisine like biryani and kebabs. Akbar's policy of sulh-i-kul promoted tolerance, while Aurangzeb was more orthodox."),
"maratha-confederacy":("Marathas were mainly farmers and soldiers from the Deccan; Shivaji's armies were paid in cash rather than land.","Maratha culture included Bhakti saints like Tukaram and Dnyaneshwar, the Pandharpur pilgrimage (wari) and Marathi literature."),
"sikh-empire":("Ranjit Singh's Punjab was prosperous farming country; Lahore was a cosmopolitan capital.","The Sikh Gurus' teachings of equality, langar and the Khalsa shaped the empire's culture."),
"kingdom-of-mysore":("Mysore's farmers grew ragi, rice, sugarcane and sandalwood; Tipu promoted silk and trade.","Mysore's Dasara festival and Carnatic music flourished under the Wodeyars."),
"east-india-company-rule":("Under the Company, peasants faced heavy revenue demands, weavers were forced to sell cloth at low prices, and famines struck Bengal (1770).","Company officials studied Indian languages (Asiatic Society, 1784), while missionaries and reformers debated social customs."),
"british-raj":("Most Indians were poor farmers; famines killed tens of millions. Railways and new cities grew; a Western-educated middle class formed.","The Raj saw the Bengal Renaissance, reform movements, the growth of Indian nationalism, and new literature in Indian languages and English."),
}
